<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Verona</h1><p class="st-meta">1136 CE – 1262 CE (approx.) · capital Verona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A city-republic on the Adige that led resistance to Frederick Barbarossa and then fell under the tyrant Ezzelino da Romano.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Verona, guarding the route from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner Pass</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, had consuls by 1136. It led the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veronese-league">Veronese League</a> of 1164 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa's</a> officials and then joined the wider <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lombard-league">Lombard League</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consuls</a> and later a podestà governed the city, whose wealth came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> trade and the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The Romanesque basilica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-zeno">San Zeno</a> was rebuilt in the 12th century. From the 1220s the city was dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezzelino-iii-da-romano">Ezzelino III da Romano</a>, a Ghibelline ally of Frederick II whose harsh rule over Verona, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-vicenza">Vicenza</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-padua">Padua</a> made him a byword for tyranny.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezzelino-iii-da-romano">Ezzelino</a>'s death in 1259 the city chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastino-i-della-scala">Mastino I della Scala</a> as its leader, and by 1262 communal government had given way to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-verona">Lordship of Verona</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> family.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/commune-of-verona.jpg" alt="Commune of Verona" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Hoppend · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Verona_cityscape_sunny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1136 CE – 1262 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Verona</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ezzelino III da Romano</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Venetian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Verona</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1199&amp;focus=commune-of-verona" aria-label="Commune of Verona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4941 575 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Verona lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5261 831L5261 831M5261 831L5261 831"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5261 831h0M5261 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1199&amp;focus=commune-of-verona">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
